      "source": [
        "1.\n",
        "\n",
        "We calculate the derivatives by treating all other variables as constants:\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial a} = b, \\quad \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial b} = a, \\quad \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial c} = 1, \\quad \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial e} = 1\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\frac{\\partial c}{\\partial a} = b, \\quad \\frac{\\partial c}{\\partial b} = a\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\frac{\\partial e}{\\partial a} = d, \\quad \\frac{\\partial e}{\\partial d} = a\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "2. \n",
        "\n",
        "In the first example, 3 different objects are created: `a`, `b` and `f`.\n",
        "\n",
        "`a.v = 3.0`, $\\quad$ `a.grad = 0.0`, $\\quad$ `a.grad_fn() = []`\n",
        "\\\n",
        "`b.v = 5.0`, $\\quad$ `b.grad = 0.0`, $\\quad$ `b.grad_fn() = []`\n",
        "\\\n",
        "`f.v = 15.0`, $\\quad$ `f.grad = 0.0`, $\\quad$ `f.grad_fn() = [(a, 5.0), (b, 3.0)]`\n",
        "\n",
        "3. \n",
        "\n",
        "First, `f.backward()` calls `f.backprop(1.0)`, then `f.grad` is set to `1.0`. After that, `a.backprop(5.0)` is called, and `a.grad` is set to `5.0`. The same happens to `b.backprop(3.0)`, which means that `b.grad = 3.0`.\n",
        "`a` and `b` will not perform additional calls, as their `grad_fn()` holds an empty list. \n",
        "\n",
        "4. \n",
        "\n",
        "First, `f.backward()` calls `f.backprop(1.0)` which then calls `a.backprop(5.0)` and `b.backprop(3.0)`."
      ]
    },
